summary:
Flame in Freefall is a web app that gathers twenty years of NASA microgravity combustion experiments. It harmonises them and makes them readable for one question: what do we really know about fire in space?

The data covers all 19 experiments of NASA’s PSI database. Their 21 tables are merged into a single table of about 3,400 tests. PSI documents, raw telemetry (FLEX-2, ACME chambers), 264 publications and 40 NTRS reports fill in missing values. The origin of every value is traced: measurement, document or team assumption.

The app offers several views:

a map of every test by oxygen and pressure;
a ranking of experiments for four habitats (ISS, lunar base, exploration atmosphere, spacesuit), based on the tests actually measured, with no arbitrary weights;
an explorer and a comparison page;
an animated simulator, where every verdict comes from cited NASA tests;
Cosmo, a mascot assistant that reads the sources and drives the interface.

The key finding: a lunar base will breathe air with 30–34% oxygen. Yet no solid material has been tested in microgravity above 22.7%, and there is no partial-gravity test at all. The app shows these gaps and what should be tested next.
